&quot;...That deathly Ai-no-Yama song was being heard with a deathly heart. The five guests and the company gathered around them were not its only audience. One more person was listening.&quot;

&quot;A half-story room projected beyond the courtyard. Room after room around it was bright; this one alone was strangely gloomy and dim. Naturally so. Here they kept women suffering from illness, women driven wild by their secret lovers,&quot;

&quot;and the room did two jobs: recovery and confinement. So its rumours were always in service. A woman had coughed blood and died here; a ghost appeared here. Such stories never stopped coming.&quot;

&quot;Before the song began, a woman in that room had ground her ink and begun a letter. Already she had used a considerable length of the paper scroll.&quot;

&quot;Furuichi&#x27;s courtesans did not make a spectacle of Katsuyama hair and elaborate outer robes. They dressed plainly, like women outside the profession. On some, that simplicity sat very well indeed,&quot;

&quot;and stories of younger men staking their lives on love for an older woman in a white waist-wrap were not uncommon.&quot;

&quot;The woman writing now was ill also. Even in that condition, she looked so thoroughly unlike a courtesan, so well did the plain appearance show her character.&quot;

&quot;Under the square vermilion lantern, her brush ran, stopped, ran again. Sometimes she wept. Then came that earlier verse—&quot;

&quot;The bells at dusk and dawn
Cry that extinction is bliss,
Yet none who hear are startled.&quot;

&quot;She dropped the brush mid-letter and listened without moving. Otama&#x27;s accompaniment flowed past in interwoven patterns.&quot;

&quot;Though flowers scatter, spring will bloom.
Though birds return to their old nests,
The journey into death has no return.&quot;
